  "version": "1.0",
  "changes_html": "<h2>About This Vintage</h2> <h2>Changes Since the Last Update</h2><p>Since the <a href=\"https://budgetlab.yale.edu/research/state-us-tariffs-april-15-2025\">April 15 report</a>:</p><ul><li>The US and China agreed to a 90-day temporary reduction in tariffs as trade talks continue. The US 125% tariff on Chinese imports announced April 2 will be reduced to 10%. The 20% broad US tariff on Chinese imports announced February 4 and increased March 4 will remain. China will lower their 125% retaliation list to 10%.</li><li>The US and the UK announced a trade deal on May 8 that included following tariff provisions, among other agreements:<ul><li>The first 100,000 UK auto imports into the US will be tariffed at 10% rather than 25%.</li><li>US tariffs on UK steel &amp; aluminum will be zeroed out.</li><li>UK tariffs on US ethanol will be zeroed out.</li></ul></li><li>The Administration <a href=\"https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/04/amendments-to-adjusting-imports-of-automobiles-and-automobile-parts-into-the-united-states/\">modified</a> the 25% auto &amp; auto part tariff to allow for a temporary offset against tariffs on parts for autos assembled in the US, equal to 3.75% of the MSRP (covering parts up to 15% of vehicle value) through April 30, 2026 and 2.5% of MSRP (covering parts up to 10% of vehicle value) through April 30, 2027. TBL modeled this as a short-run reduction in the effective tariff rate on autos.</li><li>TBL has added analysis of the long-run US sectoral output effects of tariffs.</li></ul><p>TBL analyzes tariffs on a &ldquo;real-time current policy&rdquo; basis, where policy as it stands as of date certain is assumed to continue in perpetuity, even if framed as a temporary policy.</p>",
